# harden-prod-deployment: there was no .dockerignore before this, so both
# image builds (sync, and now gateway) shipped whatever happened to be on
# disk in the build context — including, confirmed directly, the real
# .env files (SYNC_SHARED_TOKEN/VITE_SYNC_TOKEN) getting baked into the
# sync image's filesystem layer. Not a severity escalation on its own
# (the matching client-side token already ships in the public JS bundle
# by this project's own "shared token, not accounts" design — see
# bootstrap-shizi-assessment's design.md), but there is no reason to keep
# repeating it, and a real secret landing here later would be a genuine
# leak. Excluded explicitly, mirroring .gitignore's own pattern.
# A bare pattern (no `/`) only matches at the build-context ROOT in
# Docker's ignore semantics, unlike .gitignore's implicit recursion —
# confirmed the hard way twice while writing this file: first with
# nested .env files not being excluded, then with nested
# tsconfig.tsbuildinfo files not being excluded (which then confused
# `tsc -b` into thinking packages were already built when their actual
# dist/ output had correctly been excluded — a real, silently-broken
# build, not a hypothetical). Every pattern below that needs to match
# at any depth uses an explicit `**/` prefix; nothing here relies on
# bare-pattern recursion.
.env
.env.*
!.env.*.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.*.example

node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.git/
.claude/
dist/
**/dist/
spikes/
coverage/
**/coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
openspec/

# Published learner config is runtime-generated derived data, never a
# build input (harden-prod-deployment's "Immutable app, mutable config"
# decision — see docker-compose.yml's gateway service comment). Without
# this, Vite's own public/ -> dist/ copy step would silently bake
# whatever happened to be on the host at BUILD time into the image, and
# the runtime bind-mount would only mask that staleness, not prevent it.
apps/assessment/public/config.json
